{id:'solar-system',title:'Our incredible solar system',question:'What is in our solar system?',category:'space',minutes:3,tag:'Start here',words:'planets orbit earth sun solar system space astronomy',fact:'Eight planets travel around one star: our Sun.',source:'NASA Science',url:'https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/',
 levels:{'6-8':'Our Sun is a star. Eight planets travel around it. Earth is one of them! The path a planet follows around the Sun is called an orbit. Each planet has its own amazing story.', '9-11':'Our solar system is the Sun and everything held by its gravity: eight planets, their moons, dwarf planets, asteroids and comets. The four inner planets have rocky surfaces. The outer planets are much larger and very different from Earth.', '12-14':'The solar system formed about 4.6 billion years ago from a cloud of gas and dust. Gravity gathered most of the material into the Sun, while the remaining disc formed planets and smaller bodies. The system includes terrestrial planets, gas giants, ice giants and dwarf planets.'},
 sections:[['One star, many worlds','The Sun supplies the light and energy that help make life on Earth possible. Planets do not make their own visible light like stars do; they reflect sunlight.'],['A journey in order','Moving out from the Sun: Mercury, Venus, Earth, Mars, Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus and Neptune. Pluto is a dwarf planet.'],['Try thinking like a scientist','Why might a planet farther from the Sun take longer to complete an orbit? Explore the model and compare the paths.']],related:['sun','mars','algorithms']},
{id:'mars',title:'Could we visit Mars?',question:'Why is Mars red?',category:'space',minutes:3,tag:'Space mission',words:'mars red planet mission rover space',fact:'Mars has the tallest known volcano in the solar system.',source:'NASA Science',url:'https://science.nasa.gov/mars/',
 levels:{'6-8':'Mars is a rocky planet with rusty red dust. Robots called rovers explore its surface. Mars is cold, and its air is very different from the air we breathe. People would need special suits and shelters to visit.', '9-11':'Mars looks red because iron minerals in its soil have rusted. Rovers study its rocks for clues about ancient water. Its thin atmosphere is mostly carbon dioxide, so a human visitor would need oxygen, warmth and protection.', '12-14':'Mars is a cold terrestrial planet with a thin, carbon-dioxide-rich atmosphere. Evidence from channels, minerals and sedimentary rocks suggests liquid water flowed on its surface long ago. Missions investigate its past habitability; finding ancient water is not the same as finding life.'},
 sections:[['Robot explorers','Rovers carry cameras and scientific instruments. Teams on Earth plan their journeys, but a radio message takes time to travel between the planets.'],['A very different home','Mars has lower gravity than Earth and a day that is slightly longer than ours. Its year lasts about 687 Earth days.'],['Design challenge','Imagine a Mars home. How would you provide clean air, water, energy and protection from the cold? Sketch your ideas on paper.']],related:['solar-system','sun','algorithms']},
{id:'sun',title:'Meet our nearest star',question:'Is the Sun a star?',category:'space',minutes:2,tag:'Bright idea',words:'sun star light energy solar',fact:'Sunlight takes about eight minutes to reach Earth.',source:'NASA Science',url:'https://science.nasa.gov/sun/',
 levels:{'6-8':'Yes! The Sun is our nearest star. It gives Earth light and warmth. It looks much bigger than the other stars because it is much closer. Never look straight at the Sun: it can hurt your eyes.', '9-11':'The Sun is a star: a huge sphere of very hot plasma. Deep inside, hydrogen joins to form helium and releases energy. That energy eventually leaves as light and heat. Never look directly at the Sun, even during an eclipse without proper protection.', '12-14':'The Sun is a main-sequence star powered by nuclear fusion in its core. Hydrogen nuclei combine into helium, converting a small amount of mass into energy. The energy travels outward before radiating into space. Direct solar viewing can permanently damage the eyes.'},
 sections:[['Energy for life','Plants capture some sunlight through photosynthesis. Many food chains begin with plants.'],['Day and night','Earth rotates. Your part of Earth has daytime when it faces the Sun, and night when it faces away.'],['Notice safely','With a grown-up, notice how a tree’s shadow changes during the day. Stay in the shade and never stare at the Sun.']],related:['solar-system','water-cycle','patterns']},
{id:'elephants',title:'The elephant’s secret toolkit',question:'How do elephants use their trunks?',category:'animals',minutes:3,tag:'Wild neighbours',words:'elephant trunk wildlife animals kenya savannah mammal',fact:'An elephant’s trunk is a nose and upper lip combined.',source:'WWF',url:'https://www.worldwildlife.org/species/african-elephant',
 levels:{'6-8':'An elephant uses its trunk to smell, breathe, pick things up and drink. It sucks water into its trunk, then squirts it into its mouth. Big ears help it lose heat. Elephants live in families and learn from each other.', '9-11':'An elephant’s trunk is an amazingly flexible nose and upper lip. It contains many muscles and no bones. Elephants use it for food, water, scent and greeting one another. Their large ears help release body heat.', '12-14':'The elephant trunk is a muscular hydrostat: a flexible structure that moves without bones. It combines fine control with strength. African elephant species play important ecological roles, opening paths, dispersing seeds and changing vegetation as they feed.'},
 sections:[['Family matters','Elephants form strong social bonds. Older family members can help the group find water and remember routes.'],['Nature’s gardeners','Seeds can travel inside an elephant and be left in its dung. This helps some plants spread to new places.'],['Respect wildlife','Watch wild animals from a safe distance with a qualified guide. Never approach, feed or touch a wild elephant.']],related:['rhinos','rift-valley','water-cycle']},
{id:'rhinos',title:'Meet Kenya’s black rhino',question:'What is special about a black rhino?',category:'animals',minutes:3,tag:'Kenya wildlife',words:'rhino black rhino kenya wildlife conservation animals',fact:'A black rhino has a pointed upper lip for browsing leaves.',source:'WWF',url:'https://www.worldwildlife.org/species/black-rhino',
 levels:{'6-8':'Black rhinos eat leaves and twigs. Their pointed upper lip helps them grab food from bushes. They have a strong sense of smell. Kenya protects rhinos in special areas where rangers work to keep them safe.', '9-11':'Black rhinos are browsers: they feed mainly on leaves and twigs from shrubs. Their hooked upper lip helps them select food. Despite their names, black and white rhinos are both usually grey. Conservation work helps protect these threatened animals.', '12-14':'Black rhinos are critically endangered browsers. Their prehensile upper lip is adapted to selecting leaves and shoots. Habitat protection, monitoring and anti-poaching work are important to recovery. Names such as “black” and “white” are not a reliable guide to their skin colour.'},
 sections:[['Built for browsing','Compare a pointed black-rhino lip with the broad mouth of a white rhino, which mainly grazes on grass.'],['Every animal has a role','Browsing changes vegetation. Protecting a species also means caring for the habitat and the other living things it supports.'],['Become a careful observer','What could an animal’s mouth tell you about its food? Compare drawings of a rhino, elephant and giraffe.']],related:['elephants','rift-valley','wangari']},
{id:'rift-valley',title:'A giant story beneath Kenya',question:'How did the Great Rift Valley form?',category:'kenya',minutes:4,tag:'Made in Kenya',words:'kenya great rift valley earth tectonic volcano geography',fact:'The East African Rift is a place where Earth’s crust is slowly pulling apart.',source:'USGS',url:'https://www.usgs.gov/programs/earthquake-hazards/science/plate-tectonics',
 levels:{'6-8':'Earth’s hard outer layer is made of huge moving pieces. In parts of East Africa, the ground is slowly stretching. Over a very long time this has helped make valleys, mountains and volcanoes. Kenya is part of this amazing story.', '9-11':'The East African Rift formed as tectonic forces stretched and thinned Earth’s crust. Blocks of land moved along faults, helping create deep valleys. Volcanoes and lakes are part of the landscape, but the changes happen over millions of years.', '12-14':'The East African Rift is an active continental rift system. Extension thins the lithosphere and forms faults, subsiding basins and volcanic activity. The rift records complex tectonic processes operating over millions of years rather than a single sudden crack.'},
 sections:[['Slow changes, big landscapes','Tectonic plates move slowly, but their effects add up over geological time. Mountains, valleys and volcanoes tell parts of the story.'],['Look closer to home','Kenya’s rift landscape includes lakes and volcanic landforms. Each place has its own geological and ecological history.'],['Make a landscape','Use a sheet of paper to draw a valley, a lake and a volcano. Add labels explaining how each differs.']],related:['water-cycle','rhinos','wangari']},
{id:'water-cycle',title:'The never-ending water adventure',question:'Where does rain come from?',category:'science',minutes:3,tag:'Everyday science',words:'water rain clouds evaporation condensation precipitation cycle weather',fact:'The Sun helps power the water cycle.',source:'USGS Water Science School',url:'https://www.usgs.gov/special-topics/water-science-school/science/water-cycle',
 levels:{'6-8':'The Sun warms water in rivers, lakes and the ocean. Some water becomes an invisible gas called water vapour. It rises and cools. Tiny drops can form clouds. When drops grow heavy enough, water falls as rain.', '9-11':'Water moves between the atmosphere, land and oceans. Evaporation turns liquid water into vapour. Cooling can cause condensation into cloud droplets. Rain and other precipitation return water to the surface, where it can flow, soak into soil or be used by plants.', '12-14':'The water cycle transfers water among atmosphere, surface and subsurface stores. Solar energy drives evaporation and transpiration; condensation forms droplets or ice crystals. Precipitation, runoff, infiltration and groundwater flow redistribute water. There is no single starting point.'},
 sections:[['Clouds are not just vapour','Water vapour is invisible. The clouds you see contain tiny liquid drops, ice crystals, or both.'],['Water takes many paths','A raindrop might soak into the soil, reach a stream, become groundwater or be absorbed by a plant.'],['Observe without waste','Notice puddles after rain. Revisit the same spot later with a grown-up. What changed? You do not need to pour out extra water to test it.']],related:['sun','rift-valley','coral']},
{id:'algorithms',title:'Think like a coder',question:'What is an algorithm?',category:'tech',minutes:3,tag:'Future Lab',words:'code coding algorithm robot programming sequence computer technology',fact:'An algorithm is a set of steps for solving a problem or doing a task.',source:'Scratch Foundation',url:'https://www.scratchfoundation.org/learn',
 levels:{'6-8':'An algorithm is a set of steps. Think about getting ready for school: put on socks, then put on shoes! Order matters. A computer follows instructions, so we need to make the steps clear.', '9-11':'An algorithm is a clear set of steps for a task. Programmers turn algorithms into code. A sequence puts steps in order, a loop repeats them, and a condition chooses what happens next. Finding and fixing a mistake is called debugging.', '12-14':'An algorithm describes a method for solving a problem. Programs implement algorithms using constructs such as sequence, iteration and selection. Testing different inputs helps reveal edge cases. Efficiency matters, but first the algorithm must produce the intended result.'},
 sections:[['Be precise','“Go over there” may be confusing. “Move forward two squares, then turn right” gives a robot clearer instructions.'],['Mistakes help us learn','When instructions do not work, check one step at a time. A bug is a problem to investigate, not a reason to give up.'],['Your first program','Open Code a Route in Play & Learn. Build a sequence, run it, then change it until the rover reaches the star.']],related:['ai','patterns','solar-system']},
{id:'ai',title:'How does AI learn patterns?',question:'What is artificial intelligence?',category:'tech',minutes:4,tag:'Future Lab',words:'ai artificial intelligence computer learning machine technology pattern',fact:'An AI answer can sound confident and still be wrong.',source:'UNICEF',url:'https://www.unicef.org/innocenti/reports/policy-guidance-ai-children',
 levels:{'6-8':'AI is a kind of computer technology. It can find patterns and help people with some tasks. It is not a person and does not know everything. Ask a trusted grown-up when you are unsure, and keep personal information private.', '9-11':'Artificial intelligence is technology that carries out tasks such as recognising patterns or generating text. Many AI systems learn patterns from examples. Their answers can contain mistakes. AI does not replace your own thinking, a teacher or a trusted grown-up.', '12-14':'Many modern AI systems use machine learning to fit statistical patterns from training examples. Generative models create outputs using those learned patterns rather than verifying every claim. Their limitations include factual errors, bias and uncertainty. Checking sources is an essential skill.'},
 sections:[['A helper, not a human','AI can generate words that sound friendly, but it does not have human feelings or understanding. You are always free to stop using it.'],['Keep private things private','Do not share your full name, school, address, phone number, passwords or photos in an AI conversation.'],['Check a claim','Pick one surprising claim from a digital tool. Can you find the same fact in a reliable book or trusted reference?']],related:['algorithms','patterns','fractions']},
{id:'coral',title:'A city made by tiny animals',question:'Is coral an animal?',category:'ocean',minutes:3,tag:'Ocean wonders',words:'coral reef ocean sea animals marine kenya coast',fact:'Corals are animals, even though they can look like plants or rocks.',source:'NOAA Ocean Service',url:'https://oceanservice.noaa.gov/facts/coral.html',
 levels:{'6-8':'A coral reef is full of tiny animals called coral polyps. Many corals build hard skeletons. Lots of sea creatures find food and shelter around reefs. Kenya’s coast has wonderful marine life to discover.', '9-11':'Coral polyps are small animals. Many reef-building corals have tiny algae living in their tissues. The algae use sunlight to make food and share some with the coral. Over time, coral skeletons help form reefs that shelter many species.', '12-14':'Reef-building corals are colonial animals that deposit calcium carbonate skeletons. Many have a symbiotic relationship with photosynthetic algae. Heat stress can disrupt this relationship, causing bleaching. Bleached coral is stressed and may recover, but prolonged stress can kill it.'},
 sections:[['A busy neighbourhood','Reefs provide shelter and feeding areas for fish and many other animals.'],['A delicate balance','Warmer seas, pollution and physical damage can harm reefs. Protecting clean water helps marine habitats.'],['Leave only bubbles','If you visit the coast with a grown-up, do not step on coral or collect living marine animals. Observe them where they live.']],related:['water-cycle','elephants','patterns']},
{id:'wangari',title:'One woman. Millions of trees.',question:'Who was Wangari Maathai?',category:'history',minutes:3,tag:'Kenyan changemaker',words:'wangari maathai kenya trees green belt movement history people environment',fact:'Wangari Maathai received the Nobel Peace Prize in 2004.',source:'Nobel Prize',url:'https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/peace/2004/maathai/facts/',
 levels:{'6-8':'Wangari Maathai was a Kenyan scientist and changemaker. She helped people plant trees and care for the land. She showed how small actions, done together, can make a big difference.', '9-11':'Wangari Maathai founded the Green Belt Movement in Kenya in 1977. It brought communities, especially women, together to plant trees and care for the environment. In 2004 she became the first African woman to receive the Nobel Peace Prize.', '12-14':'Professor Wangari Maathai connected environmental conservation, women’s empowerment, democracy and peace. The Green Belt Movement, founded in 1977, used community tree planting as a practical response to environmental damage and livelihoods challenges. She received the 2004 Nobel Peace Prize.'},
 sections:[['A living connection','Trees can provide habitat, protect soil and contribute to local livelihoods. The right tree belongs in the right place.'],['Ideas become action','Community projects begin by listening to the people affected and working together on practical solutions.'],['Your small action','With a grown-up, learn about a tree near your home. What animals use it? Draw it and label what you notice.']],related:['rhinos','water-cycle','rift-valley']},
{id:'patterns',title:'Find the pattern. Make your own.',question:'What is a repeating pattern?',category:'art',minutes:2,tag:'Make & create',words:'art pattern creativity shapes drawing sequence',fact:'Patterns appear in art, music, maths and computer code.',source:'ZUNO original activity',url:null,
 levels:{'6-8':'A pattern is something that follows a rule. Circle, square, circle, square: what comes next? You can make a pattern using shapes, colours, sounds or movements.', '9-11':'A repeating pattern has a unit that appears again and again. Patterns can also grow or change according to a rule. Artists and designers use patterns to create rhythm and structure.', '12-14':'Patterns may be repeating, growing or recursive. Identifying the underlying rule helps us describe and predict a sequence. In visual design, symmetry, translation and rotation can build complex patterns from simple elements.'},
 sections:[['Look around','Notice patterns in a basket, a fabric, floor tiles or leaves. Describe what repeats without assuming all designs have the same meaning.'],['Create a rule','Draw a circle, two squares, then another circle and two squares. Keep repeating your unit.'],['Change one thing','Try a new colour or rotate a shape. How does the pattern feel different?']],related:['algorithms','fractions','coral']},
{id:'fractions',title:'A whole new way to share',question:'What is a fraction?',category:'math',minutes:3,tag:'Maths in real life',words:'math maths fraction half quarter equal parts sharing numbers',fact:'The parts must be equal for each one to represent the same fraction of a whole.',source:'ZUNO original maths lesson',url:null,
 levels:{'6-8':'Imagine a chapati cut into two equal pieces. Each piece is one half. Cut the same chapati into four equal pieces, and each is one quarter. Fractions help us talk about equal parts of a whole.', '9-11':'A fraction describes a number of equal parts. In 3/4, the denominator 4 tells us the whole has four equal parts, and the numerator 3 tells us we have three of them. Two quarters and one half describe the same amount.', '12-14':'Fractions represent ratios of integers, with a nonzero denominator. Equivalent fractions describe the same value: 1/2 = 2/4 = 3/6. When adding fractions with different denominators, express them with a common denominator first.'},
 sections:[['Equal parts matter','Two pieces that have different sizes are not both one half of the same chapati. The sizes, not just the number of pieces, matter.'],['Same amount, different names','Shade two of four equal squares. That is 2/4 of the shape, which is the same amount as 1/2.'],['Try it on paper','Draw a rectangle. Divide it into eight equal boxes and shade four. What fraction have you shaded? Can you name it another way?']],related:['patterns','algorithms','solar-system']}
